## Install

```
make install    # builds to ~/.local/bin/<<.Name>>
```

## The shape

Three packages, and the split between them is the point.

| | |
|---|---|
| `internal/engine` | The domain. **No terminal concepts at all** — no colour, no width, no keys, no framework. A test holds that closed. |
| `internal/tui` | The interface. Draws cells into a canvas, so the frame is its own map and a click can ask what it landed on. |
| `internal/cli` | The command tree, declared once. The CLI, the screens, `describe --json` and the context menus all read the same declaration. |

The CLI is a **peer** of the interface over the same engine, not a wrapper
around it. Neither calls the backend directly.

## Working on it

```
make check      # tests and lint: exactly what CI runs
make frames     # render every screen to a page you can look at
```

`make frames` is worth knowing about. Every screen has a golden, the goldens are
captured from a fixture rather than a live backend, and `make frames` turns the
same list into an HTML page — so a layout change is something you look at rather
than something you deduce from a diff.

To accept a deliberate change to a screen:

```
go test ./internal/tui -update-goldens
```

## What the tests hold closed

- **The screens.** A golden per state, at 132 columns and again at 80, plus a
  check that colour does not change the shape — goldens are captured uncoloured,
  so a width bug in a styled string is invisible to all of them.
- **The vocabulary.** A colour that did not come from the palette, or a
  character the glyph set does not cover, fails. The palette is the terminal's
  own sixteen ANSI indices, so <<.Name>> follows your terminal's theme. A font without a glyph draws a
  replacement box, which reads as a bug rather than as decoration.
- **The engine's ignorance.** An import that means it has learned about
  terminals fails.
- **Reachability.** Every action reachable by mouse has a keyboard path. An
  agent cannot click, and a multiplexer may eat the right-click before
  <<.Name>> ever sees it.
